{"authored" [{"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\ x+1 & =3{x}^{2}-6x+3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-7x+2 & =0 \\\\ (3x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)=\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1,$ $g(2)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2) & =\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fa965f824a37c515724383d738896c83a4ca0b50e058757bb69e1bbd63b8966"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-19201" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and the point $P(2,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fa965f824a37c515724383d738896c83a4ca0b50e058757bb69e1bbd63b8966"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-312579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-9$ |\n| $-3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=-3$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fa965f824a37c515724383d738896c83a4ca0b50e058757bb69e1bbd63b8966"} {"question" "q-342590" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342590" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(-1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(-1)=x.$ For $x\\ne 2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(-1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{x}{x-2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{x}{x-2}\\cdot (x-2) & =-1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\cancel{(x-2)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x-2)} & =-x+2 \\\\ x & =-x+2 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(-1)=1.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2}{{(1-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fa965f824a37c515724383d738896c83a4ca0b50e058757bb69e1bbd63b8966"} {"question" "q-342604" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342604" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\ x+1 & =3{x}^{2}-6x+3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-7x+2 & =0 \\\\ (3x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fa965f824a37c515724383d738896c83a4ca0b50e058757bb69e1bbd63b8966"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"} {"question" "q-342621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043784/state.json" "file_sha256" "949752bfc39597c6751c634163555e94ed7c55240a98314cd7a787afbc97873f"}] "usage" {"q-19201" [] "q-342621" []} "basis" 1645 "retractions" [[17592186064458 :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f^{′}(x), g(x)$, and $g^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1} \\cdot g)^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-